{"archetypes":[{"name":"serial-lock-correctness","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The review-and-fix.sh block swap moved the serial lock acquire/release for codex to before the cursor block, but the _SERIAL_LOCK variable is reset to empty again inside the cursor branch — verify that the lock variable scoping and release sequencing is correct after the swap, especially that the codex lock is properly released before the cursor lock is acquired.","prompt_body":"Examine the `run_coder_dispatch()` function in `skills/review-and-fix/scripts/review-and-fix.sh` after the block swap. The codex block now acquires a serial lock via `external_serial_lock_acquire _SERIAL_LOCK \"codex\"` and releases it with `external_serial_lock_release_after`, then the cursor block resets `_SERIAL_LOCK=\"\"` and acquires a new lock for cursor. Verify that this ordering does not leave a dangling lock when codex succeeds and returns early (skipping the cursor block entirely), and that the `_SERIAL_LOCK` reset at the top of the cursor block does not accidentally clobber an in-flight codex lock release. Also check whether `external_serial_lock_release_after` is truly asynchronous and whether an early `return 0` from the codex success path races with the async release. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"dispatch-idempotency","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The review-and-fix.sh swap introduces a new code structure where codex is tried unconditionally first (no presence gate) while cursor retains the cursor_launcher_load_model_args guard — verify this asymmetry is intentional and that missing-codex-binary scenarios are handled rather than silently failing.","prompt_body":"In the new `run_coder_dispatch()` in `skills/review-and-fix/scripts/review-and-fix.sh`, the codex block runs unconditionally (no `CODEX_PRESENT` gate), while the cursor fallback is still gated on `cursor_launcher_load_model_args && cursor_launcher_setup_auth_argv`. Contrast this with `lint-fix-loop.sh` where codex IS gated on `CODEX_PRESENT=true`. Check whether `run_coder_dispatch()` in `review-and-fix.sh` has any presence/availability guard for codex, or whether a missing codex binary would simply fail the `run-external-agent.sh` call and fall through to cursor — and whether that fallthrough is the intended behavior or an oversight from the block swap. Also verify the `--add-dir` flags passed to `codex exec` in `review-and-fix.sh` are correct (session tmpdir and `$PWD`) matching the launcher contract. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"test-coverage-gap","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"Test 1b was substantially simplified from a full stub-cursor integration test to a simple non-git-cwd exit-2 assertion — verify that the new test actually distinguishes the codex-default from claude-default as claimed, and that no other tests in the file now implicitly depend on the removed cursor-stub infrastructure.","prompt_body":"Review the new Test 1b in `skills/implement/scripts/test-step2-dispatch.sh` and its `.md` counterpart. The old test used a cursor stub binary and asserted `STATUS=bailed REASON=stub-bailed TOOL=cursor` to prove cursor was the default; the new test asserts exit=2 from a non-git cwd. Verify the new test actually proves codex is the default (not just that non-git-cwd exits 2 for any non-claude coder), and check whether test comment blocks in later tests (1c, 3b, 3b2, etc.) that previously referenced Test 1b's cursor-stub setup now have stale references or rely on variables/files that the removed cursor-stub block created. Specifically check whether `$STUB_BIN_1B`, `$STUB_CURSOR_1B`, or `$STUB_MANIFEST_PATH` variables appear in any later test block. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
